A||||6||GarethWright||AC Compressor Replacement||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||04-04-2001||06:33 PM||||Hello all, you can tell its getting warm out, the AC questions all pop up, so heres mine:<BR>I am pretty sure that my AC compressor is dead, so I am hoping to replace it myself, I can get a new compressor and clutch for $190.<BR>Can I discharge the system myself or do I have to take it to a mechanic?<BR>How do I discharge the system If I can?<BR>What other little things should I be aware of when I do this?<BR>I figure now is as good a time as any to go to 134 freon, so what else needs replacing? just the accumulator?<BR>Thanks<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-04-2001||07:10 PM||||You must be licensed to handle R12. Once the system is purged you can legally work on it yourself.<P>You do not need a license for R134.<P>there are also some notes in my cave that may be of use.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||04-04-2001||08:49 PM||||I replaced my compressor last year and converted to R134a.  ('85 SE 4-cylinder)  In addition to a compressor, I replaced accumulator, orifice tube, and o-rings in all fittings I could reach.  I also flushed the lines.  And of course you have to replace charge fittings to use R134a.<P><BR>Pitfalls:<P>Compressor was a pain to replace; there is <I>just barely</I> enough room to maneuver around the exhaust.  It would be easier now that I have Rodney's smaller catalytic converter.<P>New compressor probably doesn't come with switches (fan and high-pressure cutoff).  Some replacement switches have reversed connectors; that is, the fan switch connector fits the high-pressure cutoff switch and <I>vice versa</I>.<P>I had trouble finding a workable charging port adapter.<P><BR>However, it cools well.<P><BR>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||04-04-2001||11:22 PM||||If the system is still under pressure what makes you believe the compressor is dead?  Does it grind or squeal?<P>Which engine do you have?<P>I replaced my compressor with a new Delco, the accumulator, the orifice tube, and all the o-rings when I installed the new engine.  I also flushed out the lines when I had everything apart.<P>I've never tried to get the compressor off either of my 85 v-6s while the engine was still in the car yet.  I'll be replacing the compressor on the 85GT soon.  Not looking forward to it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GarethWright||04-05-2001||12:04 AM||||oops knew I forgot something  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> its an 87 v6.<P>I am not sure exactly what is wrong, a brand new belt on there(yes, it's the right size  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) lasts about 2 days before wearing so thin that it chatters and squeels.  During those 2 days, I have working AC though.  I have been told that if the Clutch were siezed, it would run the compressor All the time.<BR>So the only other solution I can see, is that the compressor itself is jamming up while turned on, and the belt(still turning) wears on the pulley(not turning) until it is so loose and thin that it just squeels and chatters.  does that sound right?<BR>If you have any other ideas, I would be glad to hear them, it sounds like this job isn't an easy one.<BR>Thanks again,<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||04-05-2001||07:45 AM||||I don't believe this job is beyond the capabilities of someone with basic mechanical talents and a lot of patience.<P>On my 85GT I had to replace the bracket that supports the dogbone strut.  It is bolted to the front head (or maybe to the block) with three bolts that also hold the A/C support bracket on.  I removed the battery and its splash shield to get better access to the three bolts.  When I removed them the whole A/C bracket was more accessible.<P>I believe I'll tackle the compressor from that angle.<P>From underneath I'll remove the metal heat shield to get better access to the belt tensioning bolt.  Remove the passenger side wheel well splash curtain to see the compressor pulley end better.<P>If you have access to a pressure washer it would make life easier if you blast everything clean before you start.  You could even do this at a carwash.  Just remove the splash shields first and place the car on jack stands to reach underneath and spray the compressor. <P>The orifice tube runs along the bottom edge of the front firewall underneath the spare tire.  I removed the spare tire tub to get to it.  The front brake tubing is connected to the tub underneath and must be disconnected to get the tub out of the way.<P>Hope this helps.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||GarethWright||04-05-2001||02:30 PM||||Ok so you think it Is the whole compressor that needs replacing, not just the clutch or something?<BR>Thanks for all the great info.<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
